  <p>That's 4 exports, 9 uploads, 1 rename and 3 pastes. Seventeen moves for one post on two networks, counted on this example in October 2026 rather than measured across teams. Your count will differ, and it's worth making on your own last campaign.</p>

  <p>Every move is a chance to grab the wrong file. "final_v3" and "final_v3_crop" look alike in a download folder at six in the evening.</p>

  <p>The cost in time is harder to pin down, and nobody has measured it for marketing teams. No statistics office, trade body or peer-reviewed study has published the time lost to file hand-offs between marketing tools. The closest measurement comes from other desks.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">Workers in 20 teams at three large companies switched between apps and websites nearly 1,200 times a day and spent just under four hours a week getting reoriented, about 9 percent of their working time.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: study in a business management magazine, August 2022, 137 users logged for up to five weeks, mostly finance, HR and supply-chain jobs. A market claim: the authors' company sells the measuring software.</p>

  <p>Take it as a direction for your team rather than a number. The jobs were back office, and the people who measured it sell software for the problem.</p>

  <h2>Why does the approval record matter more than the feature list?</h2>

  <p>Because in a stack, nobody approves the post that goes out.</p>

  <p>Look at the trace again. The reviewer approves a picture and a block of text in the approval tool. Someone then rebuilds the post in the scheduler, from files that came out of the file store. If the wrong crop gets picked, or the copy is pasted from the draft before the edit, the approval no longer covers what's published. Nothing in the stack connects the two.</p>

  <p>The record is split as well. Earlier versions sit in the editor's history. Comments live in the approval tool, or in somebody's inbox. The file store keeps whatever was uploaded last. Rebuilding who approved what, three months later, means opening four tools and trusting the file names.</p>

  <p>In hubStudio, a post is sent for validation from the post itself, with its copy and a link to each of its pictures. The validator approves it or sends it back with a comment. A new version lands on the same thread as v2, then v3. Nothing on the thread can be deleted, so the record of who approved what is permanent. And while the post waits, it's locked: nobody can change, delete or publish it until the validator decides.</p>

  <p><a href="/resources/how-to/social-media-client-approval">The client approval how-to</a> walks through the round trip step by step.</p>

  <h2>How does the money work in each model?</h2>

  <p>A stack bills tool by tool. Each one has its own plan, monthly or annual, and many in the category price by seat, so the bill grows with people times tools. A designer who needs the editor, the file store and the scheduler holds three seats.</p>

  <p>Then there's the software nobody opens.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">Marketers reported using 42 percent of the capabilities in their marketing technology stack in 2022, down from 58 percent in 2020. The 2025 edition of the same survey, of 413 marketing technology leaders, put utilization at 49 percent.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: industry analyst firm surveys, published October 2022 (324 marketers, May and June 2022) and 2025 (fielded June to August 2025). Self-reported shares of capability used, with no count of tools behind them.</p>

  <p>They're shares of capability with no money attached, and they cover whole marketing stacks rather than social tools alone. Still, two editions of one survey put use at under half of what was bought.</p>

  <p>hubStudio is paid a different way. There's no subscription and no seat fees, and creating an account costs nothing. A team tops up one shared, prepaid balance held in real currency, by card, Alipay or WeChat Pay. Each run is charged at the price shown before it runs, a failed run isn't charged, and the balance doesn't expire. An admin can set a daily spending limit per creator.</p>

  <p>Which comes out cheaper depends on how much you make. A seat costs the same in a quiet month as in a launch month. A run is paid only when it happens, so a team that renders a lot pays for a lot of runs, and a team that renders little pays little.</p>

