  <h2>What does one app cover, and what does it leave out?</h2>

  <p>Run the jobs in the trace against the app. Here is what hubStudio covers, as of October 2026, and where it stops. <a href="/app">The hubStudio app</a> page lists every module.</p>

  <p>On the making side, Explore lists the image and video engines of several makers, and the Image studio and Video studio run them, with History keeping every render, its prompt and its engine. The Image editor frames a picture for each network and handles crop, light and color, text, arrows and a logo, free in the browser.</p>

  <p>The Assets Library holds the team's files in folders, with tags, versions and search. Campaigns gathers one launch's files under one name without copying anything.</p>

  <p>Validation carries the approval thread. Then the publishing modules post to LinkedIn, Instagram, Facebook, TikTok (in Beta) and X, now or on a schedule, to up to 20 accounts per post, through a queue that retries when a network fails for a moment. For YouTube, the app writes the title and description; you publish the video yourself in YouTube Studio.</p>

  <p>Now the gaps. There's no API, no connector to a digital asset management system (DAM) or a product information system (PIM), no brand kit, no single sign-on and no mobile app. A team whose process runs through any of those is better served by a stack, or by handing the work to a studio.</p>

  <p><a href="/app/publish">The Publish page of the hubStudio app</a> and <a href="/app/library">the Assets Library page</a> show both modules in detail.</p>

  <h2>When does a stack of specialist tools still make sense?</h2>

  <p>When one person does one job all day, and does it deeply.</p>

  <p>A retoucher compositing product shots for hours needs a dedicated retouching tool, not a crop-and-caption editor. A motion designer building title sequences frame by frame needs a full motion suite. The app's editors cover what social formats need; they don't try to replace those desks.</p>

  <p>The same goes for systems. A catalog that lives in a PIM, or a brand library that lives in a DAM wired to the ecommerce site, pulls the stack toward those systems. The app has no connector to them.</p>

