  <h2>Questions buyers ask about one app against a tool stack</h2>

  <h3>Is an all-in-one social media tool better than separate tools?</h3>

  <p>For a team that makes and publishes its own posts, usually yes. What you gain is fewer exports, uploads and re-approvals, plus an approval that covers the post that actually goes out. Separate tools win when one specialist job, such as heavy retouching or motion design, needs more depth than an all-in-one app offers.</p>

  <h3>What are the hidden costs of using many marketing tools?</h3>

  <p>Three of them. Seats multiply across people and tools. Some of what you pay for never gets opened. And time goes into moving files from one tool to the next. Analyst surveys put use at 42 to 49 percent of a marketing stack's capabilities. No neutral study has measured the hand-off time for marketing teams, so count the exports and uploads on one of your own posts.</p>

  <h3>How do I keep track of versions across several tools?</h3>

  <p>With difficulty. Versions end up in the editor, comments in the approval tool, and the latest file in the file store. Pick one place as the record and make every approval happen there. In hubStudio, versions sit on one Validation thread, nothing on it can be deleted, and a post waiting for approval is locked until someone decides.</p>

  <h3>Do all-in-one apps charge per seat?</h3>

  <p>Some price per seat and some per account, so check the pricing model before the feature list. hubStudio does neither: there's no subscription and no seat fee. A team tops up a shared prepaid balance, each run is charged at the price shown before it runs, and a failed run isn't charged. Adding a teammate adds a login, not a seat.</p>

  <h3>When does a specialist tool make more sense?</h3>

  <p>When one person spends the whole day on one job that needs depth, like compositing and retouching or motion graphics. A catalog that has to stay in sync with a product information system counts too. Build the stack around that desk, and let the rest of the social work run in one place.</p>

  <h3>Can one app create, approve and publish social content?</h3>

  <p>Yes. In hubStudio a post is drafted, given pictures rendered or edited in the same place, sent for validation, then published or scheduled to LinkedIn, Instagram, Facebook, TikTok (Beta) and X. YouTube is the exception: the app writes the title and description, and you publish the video in YouTube Studio yourself.</p>
